1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
And now it's the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,000
You know, when I go camping, I always throw a couple of coins under my pillow.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Yeah, they're my sleeping quarters.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,500
Yeah, it hasn't gotten any better, has it?

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Hey, this is podcast number 353..

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Hey,

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Whoa,

8
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
This is Clark Green and another briefish visit this week

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
as we cast about for what in the world to do with this podcast,

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
But we'll keep up with things.

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
A lot of emails over the summer had some good discussions with folks who were asking advice,

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
And I want to talk briefly today about two key concepts that may help you be a better scouter,

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
because they condense into one talk several email questions I had this summer

15
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
and things that, at least in my judgment, are really important to our work.

16
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The first idea is that scouting is non-academic, experiential learning,

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
And I know I've talked about this many times before, but I think it's always important to emphasize-

18
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
and I get questions about this all the time.

19
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
I think every one of us should look at our programs

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
and judge whether or not we're employing academic or experiential methods.

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Now, you may be tired of me saying this and drawing this distinction,

22
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
but we have to guard against applying the methods of schools and teachers to scouting.

23
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Scouting is not a class, It is a game

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
And there are rules to the game and they're deceptively simple.

25
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
but turning scouting into an academic exercise is like trying to play football with a basketball.

26
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Now, you can actually do that and it might be kind of fun,

27
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
but you definitely sense that something is not quite right.

28
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
You're not exactly using the right methodology or the right tools.

29
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
So I want you to try something.

30
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:31,000
The next time you want to help your scouts obtain a given set of skills,

31
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,000
aim at creating an opportunity to learn and use that skill. at the same time,

32
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
You want to learn about map reading.

33
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Don't sit down and talk about maps.

34
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Hand them a map and ask them to follow it, to discover what the symbols mean

35
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
and how a map works and how to find directions.

36
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
And maybe a compass would be a handy thing to have.

37
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:51,000
How do compasses work?

38
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
If you want them to learn to build a fire and cook something outdoors,

39
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
hunger is a great incentive and scouts are always hungry.

40
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
So how do you build a fire? How do you cook outdoors?

41
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:02,000
What if it's raining?

42
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
What do you use for tinder and kindling and things like that?

43
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Let them discover. Let them work things out on their own

44
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
And when they get stuck, don't share information,

45
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Don't say, well, this is what you do.

46
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Ask questions that lead them towards the answer.

47
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Do you think that kind of tinder is going to work very well,

48
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Because you've tried three or four times and it hasn't worked very well?

49
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
What other things could you possibly use?

50
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Have you tried using this? Have you tried that

51
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Those kinds of simple questions help them get unstuck

52
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
and can move them on to the next thing?

53
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
And don't use your questions in a snide manner that derides

54
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
or demeans a lack of knowledge.

55
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Encourage and build confidence in what they do know

56
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
to help guide them towards the answers that they haven't found yet.

57
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Now, in some ways, this kind of experiential learning

58
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
is really slow and tiresome,

59
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
especially when you know everything right.

60
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
You're smarter than your scouts, I hope.

61
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Maybe, maybe sometimes,

62
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
But you know what I'm talking about.

63
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
It could be really tiresome because it's so much easier

64
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
just to sit down and give them a handout

65
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
and say: this is the way you do it,

66
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
But that way, that experiential way of learning things,

67
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
is infinitely more exciting and fun and engaging.

68
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
It might be frustrating at times for everybody,

69
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
but, like I said, encourage and build confidence

70
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
when they become frustrated.

71
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
I tell you what they learn doing this will last longer,

72
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
because they don't only learn this in their brain, right,

73
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
They learn it in their hands and their feet.

74
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
And besides the satisfaction your scouts will derive

75
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
out of figuring something out on their own,

76
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
you will be achieving our main goal, right?

77
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
The thing that everything about scouting is aimed at,

78
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
and that is developing character

79
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
by applying the patrol system-

80
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
And yes, the patrol system. that doesn't seem right here, does it?

81
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
How does that apply to learning?

82
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Well, scouts do things together,

83
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
And in the thousands of small interactions

84
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
that they have in doing those things together,

85
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
they learn more about themselves and the world around them

86
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
than they would in any traditional classroom environment.

87
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
We are in the opportunity business.

88
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
That's what scouts do.

89
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,000
We create opportunities for young people to do things.

90
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
And that leads me into the second point

91
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
that I want to share with you today.

92
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
The attitude with which we approach our work

93
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
is really all important,

94
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,000
because we're believers in the ability of young people

95
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
to make our world a better place.

96
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
We do our work in a relentlessly positive atmosphere.

97
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
that begins with the scout oath and law,

98
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Because the scout oath and law are about who to be

99
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
and what to do not, who not to be

100
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
and what not to do.

101
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
So look at the scout oath for a moment.

102
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
On my honor, I will do my best-

103
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Not anyone else's honor, but mine.

104
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
On my honor,

105
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Not anyone else's best, but my best.

106
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
As scouts, we don't invite comparisons,

107
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
but measuring ourselves

108
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
against our own internal standard of best.

109
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
And if we're going to do that-

110
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
scouts being different standards

111
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
from their family, their history,

112
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
their religious upbringing,

113
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
and when we are put in the position of questioning

114
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
a scout's behavior, we ask them to explain

115
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
their inner standard,

116
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
The thought process that define their action.

117
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Everything we say or do points back at us, right,

118
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
What's driving and motivating us?

119
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
What are we basing our actions on?

120
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
And if you really listen,

121
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
you have the opportunity to share things with them

122
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
that may help them look at the world in a different way,

123
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Or you may learn to look at the world in a different way.

124
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Everything we say or do points back at us.

125
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
What's driving and motivating us?

126
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Categorizing or measuring are, in one way,

127
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
a form of judgment that is inherently divisive and isolating.

128
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Listening is a way of understanding

129
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
rather than making judgments.

130
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Insighting divisions is the weakest form of leadership.

131
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
It's inhumane and damaging to everything

132
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
the scout oath and law represent,

133
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
And we incite divisions

134
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
when we try to categorize scouts

135
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
or measure one scout against another.

136
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Listening and trying to understand the motivations

137
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
of others brings us together

138
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and draws people towards us

139
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
rather than pushes them away from us.

140
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
So, on my honor, I will do my best

141
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
to do my duty to God in my country.

142
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
and to be convinced of the rightness

143
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
of your own convictions,

144
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
of what is properly religious or patriotic,

145
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Can lead us towards a divisive approach

146
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
that invites us to condemn others

147
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
who don't act or believe as we do.

148
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Understanding that people of good will

149
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
can look at the world differently

150
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
can bring us closer together.

151
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
We don't have to erase differences,

152
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
only to respect them

153
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and seek the common ideals

154
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
those differences express.

155
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
That's really the test of good will, isn't it?

156
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Looking for the ideals of the scout oath and law

157
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:22,000
being expressed,

158
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
even when the means of expression

159
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
may not be one we would follow personally,

160
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Because you know I mean, there's no more

161
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
lonely or inadequate form of leadership

162
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
than demanding that everybody else

163
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,000
be like you, believe like you and act like you.

164
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
I'm going to repeat myself by saying

165
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
we need to look for the ideals

166
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
of the scout oath and law

167
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,000
being expressed,

168
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
even when the means of expression

169
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
we may have a difference with

170
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
It may not be something we would follow personally,

171
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
And doing a real, honest investigation of that

172
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
is something that draws people together.

173
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
So, on my honor, I will do my best

174
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
to do my duty to God in my country

175
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
to obey the scout law.

176
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
And the scout law begins with

177
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
these three words, and these three words are real important.

178
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
A scout is.

179
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
A scout is

180
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Not a boy or a girl or a young person or a kid

181
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
or an adult, but a scout,

182
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Being a good scout, is the ideal

183
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
towards which we all strive.

184
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,000
The scout law is a statement of aspirations.

185
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
These are things that I will strive to be.

186
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
I will strive to be a scout,

187
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
and this is what a scout is.

188
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,000
This may not be what I am right now,

189
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
because I know that that's true of myself, right,

190
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
That's true of you.

191
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
You don't embody perfectly each one of those ideals,

192
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
but you strive to be. a scout is.

193
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
The scout law is in a yardstick

194
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
we use to measure scouts.

195
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,000
No, it's an encouragement to be fully awake

196
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
and aware as a human being

197
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and contribute to your culture,

198
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
your society, your country, your world,

199
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,000
your family and to be true to yourself

200
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
On my honor. I will do my best

201
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
to do my duty to God in my country

202
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
to obey the scout law,

203
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
to help other people at all times.

204
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Good leaders find a way to help others.

205
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
That's what we do.

206
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
That kind of leadership is what we want to model for our scouts.

207
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
We don't try to tear others down,

208
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
We don't try and point out the differences

209
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
that we have with others.

210
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
We don't bully them or berate them,

211
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
but we help them.

212
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
We help other people win

213
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Well, at all times. that's the default setting.

214
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
I'm reminded of Fred Rogers' idea

215
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
of looking for the helpers

216
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
to calm a child's fears

217
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
in the eyes of others in times of tragedy and loss.

218
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
You may have heard this before. right,

219
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Fred Rogers, that's Mr Rogers.

220
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
He said: when it's upsetting

221
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
to watch things on television

222
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
like earthquakes and floods

223
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
and things like that,

224
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
we encourage children to look for the helpers.

225
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
Let's be that kind of helper

226
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
when there's something tragic

227
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
And tragedy and loss

228
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
doesn't have to be confined

229
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
to floods and hurricanes

230
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and earthquakes

231
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
or earthquakes and things like that.

232
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
It's what we go through,

233
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
the difficulties we go through in our daily lives,

234
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
In our families,

235
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
in our cultures,

236
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
in our schools,

237
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
in our work.

238
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
there are, like many tragedies all the time.

239
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
Be the helper,

240
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Be the one who would be an example to children

241
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
to quell their fears

242
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
of that tragedy happening to them.

243
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
On my honor,

244
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
I will do my best to do my duty to God

245
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
and to people at all times,

246
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
to keep myself physically strong,

247
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
mentally awake

248
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
and morally straight,

249
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
To keep myself

250
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
Nobody else but me

251
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Myself.

252
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
We tend to judge ourselves

253
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
pretty harshly, I think,

254
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
If we're true to ourselves, right,

255
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
Especially when we're young,

256
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
Our inner criticism is not always fair.

257
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
It's not always our best friend.

258
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
So we have to practice the scout law

259
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
for ourselves and loyal

260
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
and friendly and courteous.

261
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
We have to be kind

262
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
to ourselves as well as

263
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
kind to other people.

264
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Otherwise we're not going to get very far right.

265
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
Weak and ineffectual leaders are consumed

266
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
with the judgment

267
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
of the way others live their lives

268
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and express themselves,

269
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
And we don't want to be weak and ineffectual.

270
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
We need to remind ourselves in the scout oath

271
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
we are keeping ourselves physically strong,

272
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Keeping ourselves

273
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
mentally awake and ourselves

274
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
morally straight,

275
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
And never to use that encouragement

276
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
as a way to divide

277
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
and to look down on others.

278
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
When I see a leader who's condemning

279
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
and judgmental, I feel sorry for them honestly,

280
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Because I know all too well

281
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
what that must feel like on the inside.

282
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
I've been there before

283
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
To be so empty and unhappy with yourself

284
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
that you have to draw divisions

285
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
amongst others. I mean, that's what bullies do, right,

286
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
In the vain attempt to cover their own

287
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
prophecies, They draw divisions

288
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
and they're mean and berating

289
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
to other people. That kind of

290
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
transparent self-hatred is painful.

291
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
It's so painful

292
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
and I feel so embarrassed for them.

293
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
So we want to look

294
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
at the scout oath and law as

295
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
something that we return to

296
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
all the time to define

297
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
our attitudes and action.

298
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Scouters champion common ideals

299
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
that bring scouts together

300
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
that doesn't divide

301
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
them up into divisions of

302
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
self-righteous people but

303
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
into true forces for good,

304
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
for positive, helpful

305
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
friendships, to expressions

306
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
of kindness and courtesy.

307
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
And that work

308
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
for a scouter is difficult

309
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
and it's never done. It takes

310
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
infinitely more effort and leadership

311
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
to bring people together in a spirit of love

312
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
and goodwill than it does

313
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
to divide them with angry, judgment

314
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
and incitement. If you've worked

315
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
with young people, you know how easy it would be

316
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
to incite them to join forces

317
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
in anger and judgment against nearly

318
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
anything And you know,

319
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
frankly, that's the chumps way out, That's

320
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
shooting fish in a barrel

321
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
when you're working with young people. It's how

322
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
demagogues and dictators have reeked so much

323
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
pain on the world, because

324
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
the emotions

325
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
and the proclivities

326
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
of human nature that are

327
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
the opposite of the scout law

328
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
are very strong and they're easy

329
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
to incite. But the saving grace

330
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
for us as scouters is that young people are

331
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
just as ready to be

332
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
led in a positive direction

333
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
based on love and goodwill,

334
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
And when they are headed

335
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
in that direction they'll do great things

336
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
and become great leaders themselves

337
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
if we create the

338
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
opportunities for that

339
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
and encourage them to understand

340
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
the ideals of scouting

341
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
that are expressed in the scout

342
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
oath and law

343
